  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 246 AM MDT TUE SEP 22 2026\nSharply cooling temperatures, gusty winds, and precipitation to\nthe west and north are all slated for later this week and \nweekend as a strong coastal trough and associated cold front \nmigrate inland. The cold frontal passage itself will occur early\nFriday afternoon in the latest synoptic model runs, with good \nagreement even amongst forecast systems and physical/AI cores. \nPre- frontal winds gust 25 to 35 mph across the area that \nmorning, winds slightly weaken around sunrise, and then grow \neven stronger in peak afternoon heating and in the post-frontal \nenvironment with gusts up to 30 to 45 mp. Winds remain strong \n30 to 40 mph through Friday night in the Snake Plain. For most \nof the area, the strong frontal winds are the primary forecast \nconcern. Showers along and behind the front will be limited to \nsouth- central Oregon, northeastern Oregon, and north-central \nIdaho. Southwestern Idaho remains drier due to the trough \nswinging northward while it moves inland. In areas expected to \nsee showers, weak embedded thunderstorms are possible too. \nNotable rainfall amounts are limited to higher terrain near the \nBlue Mountains in Oregon and the West-Central Mountains in \nIdaho, around 0.1 inches through the frontal passage. Forecast \namounts could rise if the troughs track moves further south, or \nif instability increases and showers grow stronger. Temperatures\ncool about 5-10 degrees as the front moves through on Friday, \nand about 10-15 degrees Saturday behind the front.\n\nCooler temperatures stick around through the weekend and into\nearly next week as a trailing trough amplifies off the coast.\nMaximum temperatures Saturday through Tuesday will be around\nroom temperature in lower elevations and in the low 60s to high\n50s in higher terrain towns. Some mild northwesterly gustiness\nis expected to persist through the weekend each afternoon. The\ntrailing trough arrives Sunday evening, looking much weaker than\nthe trough Friday. Precipitation is possible Sunday through\nMonday in NE Oregon and N-central Idaho again, with 20 to 30 mph\ngusts possible in the Snake Plain at the same time. Models\ndiverge beyond Tuesday.",
